{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he sent… to Balaam…</b> It was necessary to mention his father's name <b>son of Beor</b>, and also to mention his place <b>Pethor, which…</b> <b>the land of the children of his people</b>, to inform you of the alacrity that Balak the wicked exercised in this mission, so that it would reach completion immediately and at once. Therefore he specified to the messengers and said <b>son of Beor</b>, lest Balaam be confused with another Balaam; and so that they would not be delayed searching for him, he indicated to them his location and said <b>Pethor</b>; and lest they suspect a different place whose name is Pethor, he said <b>upon the river</b>; and he further informed them that that land is <b>the land of the children of his people</b>, and they all knew him and also knew the route to take to get there, for sometimes the man might not be found in his house, and they would inform them of the place where they would find him." "<b>To call for him.</b> He was cunning at the outset of the mission's message, saying that the reason he was sending for him was for Balaam's own benefit — that he wished to do him a kindness — and his intention in this was that he would run after his own advantage without any delay." "<b>Behold, a people has come out, etc.</b> One must understand his saying <b>behold</b>, and also his saying <b>has come out</b> — for was it not known to the entire world that Israel had come out, and all the more so to Balaam, that it was necessary to inform him? Also, his saying <b>behold, it covers</b> — he should have said ״and it covered, etc.״" "And the matter will be explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 1:9), who said that Pharaoh had three counselors when he said concerning Israel ״Come, let us deal wisely with him״, and one of them was Balaam, and it was he who counseled evil against them, and with his sorceries he established a spell so that no slave could flee from Egypt, and it was assumed with certainty that the people of Israel would never leave Egypt. Therefore he sent to say to him <b>Behold, a people has come out of Egypt</b> — meaning, the opposite of what was known, and his sorceries' power was nullified. And it is possible that Balaam had been assuring Balak that Israel would never leave there, and therefore he said to him ״Behold, it has come out.״ And his saying <b>Behold, it covers the eye of the land</b> — he intended this as well against what he had counseled against them with his evil counsels, to diminish them and also to kill their males and destroy their sons. Behold, this is the opposite of those things: not only were they not diminished, but on the contrary they multiplied exceedingly, and that is what he said: <b>Behold, it covers the eye of the land.</b>" "<b>And he sits opposite me.</b> By this he intended to say that it is recognizable from their deeds that they are setting their faces against me to dispossess me. And you will find that our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:7) said that Israel would make themselves appear armed with weapons against Moab, on account of the audacity of the one inscribed in the Name, as is known; and from this he sensed that their intention was to do them harm. And Balak's reason for sending these words in his mission to Balaam was because he was concerned that Balaam would say: Why should I go there, when I am able to do the deeds that are in my power to do in the place where he is? Balak was also concerned that Balaam would sense through his sorceries that they would neither harm nor destroy within the territory of Moab, and that he would not wish to come, and from there would send word to them that the people would not besiege them nor engage them in battle. Therefore he prefaced at the beginning of the mission and said <b>Behold, a people</b>, etc., <b>Behold, it covers</b>, etc., to say: You cannot rely on matters concerning this people based on what sorcery will report about them, for you said they would not leave — and they left; and counsel against them likewise did not stand, just as your counsel to diminish them did not stand, for behold, it covers, etc.; and you say to me that they will not wage war against Moab — and behold, I see that it is contrary to this, for behold, he sits opposite me. Therefore you must not rely on what you perceive through your knowledge, and come, do come."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:17.024889680Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}